A55 — the cursor rides the daemon's cursor plane

With the cursor shown, every cursor motion was a full composite and a new
scan-out flip: drm_cursor_plane is off on gamenative-drm, so a frame with a
cursor layer always takes the composite path (RP6, Dave the Diver at a 60 cap:
70-76 SurfaceFlinger presents/s instead of 60, +2.8 % gamescope CPU, +500
wakeups/s, and LSFG pacing wrecked by the extra source frames). The plane was
off because the daemon's cursor plane was a stub: capped at 64x64 so libliftoff
could not park the game there, and never presented.

A daemon with a real cursor plane accepts the private client cap GNCL
(0x474E434C): it presents the cursor plane on a layer of its own above the
scan-out layer, answers DRM_CAP_CURSOR_WIDTH/HEIGHT with its limit (256), and
refuses any larger framebuffer on that plane, so the game can never land on it.

- init_drm sets GNCL under gamenative-drm before reading the cursor caps. When
  it is accepted, drm_cursor_plane is turned on and the cursor is scanned out on
  the plane; when it is refused (an older daemon), nothing changes.
- The direct path (the game's buffer on the primary plane) needs nothing more:
  a cursor-only paint commits the same primary framebuffer with the cursor
  plane moved, which the daemon turns into a cursor-layer update and no frame.
- The composite path (FSR/NIS/SGSR, filters, looks, a game buffer the primary
  plane refuses) composites every layer except the cursor and commits the
  composite with the cursor on its plane. A paint whose only cause is a cursor
  motion re-commits the last such composite instead of compositing again
  (not with a fade, blur, ReShade or composite debug on). If the two-plane
  commit is refused, that frame composites the cursor in as before, and the
  same cursor image is not offered to the plane again.
- A cursor motion with the cursor on the plane is a repaint request like any
  other (picked up on the next vblank, painted on every flip type), but it is
  recorded as a cursor repaint instead of setting hasRepaint: a motion serial
  the paint records, so a motion during a paint stays pending. A paint whose
  only cause is a cursor motion is flagged cursor-only, and the backend takes
  the cursor-only path (same primary framebuffer or last composite, cursor
  plane moved, no composite).

Logs: "cursor: daemon cursor layer on (GNCL accepted, max 256x256),
drm_cursor_plane=1" or "... off (GNCL rejected: ...)" once at start, and
"cursor: composite under the cursor plane: composites=N cursor-only reuses=M".

